\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces GHXTox inference architecture. The peptide sequence is processed by a task-adaptive 1D expert and a pLDDT-aware multi-scale 3D expert; their probabilities are combined for peptide-toxicity prediction.}}{29}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces GHXTox discrimination and probability reliability. (A--C) Test1 ROC, precision--recall, and reliability curves; (D--F) corresponding Test2 curves. Orange, blue, and navy denote the task-adaptive 1D expert, the 3D structural expert, and probability fusion, respectively. Gray dashed lines denote chance, prevalence, or ideal-reference behavior.}}{29}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Complementarity of the 1D and 3D experts on Test1 and Test2. (A) Expert-outcome groups and their toxic fractions. (B) Conditional rescue rates with 95\% Wilson intervals. (C) Prespecified 3D-only versus 1D-only attribute effects with 95\% bootstrap intervals; none remained significant after Benjamini--Hochberg correction.}}{30}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Protocol-separated structural ablations. (A, B) Previous and pLDDT-aware 3D experts on Test1 and Test2. (C) Training-OOF comparison without and with chemical-site information.}}{30}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces OOF-frozen selective prediction and benchmark transfer. (A, B) Training-OOF accepted error and MCC across coverage levels. (C--E) Realized coverage, accepted error, and MCC after applying the unchanged cutoff to Test1 and Test2. Shading and error bars denote the manuscript-defined 95\% bootstrap intervals; no benchmark-specific cutoff was recomputed.}}{31}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Structure-confidence and chemical-site sensitivity. (A) Mean pLDDT versus the peptide-level structural gate. (B) Within-peptide pLDDT--node-gate associations. (C) Category-level and (D) largest individual-site deletion effects. Deletion effects denote model sensitivity, not experimentally verified toxicophores.}}{32}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Representative expert disagreements. (A) Structural evidence rescues a toxic decision missed by the 1D expert. (B) Opposing structural evidence reverses a correct 1D decision. Bars show expert and fused probabilities relative to the frozen threshold; the cases are illustrative, not causal biological evidence.}}{32}{figure.caption.15}%
